output:
{
  "title": "Before you make this load-bearing: four constraints that were measured, not guessed",
  "purpose": "To get an explicit, recorded answer on each of the four operational constraints before this service carries anyone's work but yours. The answers become the deploy checklist for this environment — replica count, proxy timeout, CDN behaviour, and whether participant links being capabilities is acceptable here. If any answer is 'not checked', the deploy waits.",
  "resumeHint": "Once answers are in, reconcile them against the Deployment manifest and env: replicas, MAX_AWAIT_MS, LINK_TTL_HOURS, and the canonical /mcp path. Then deploy.",
  "participants": [
    {
      "name": "Deploy owner",
      "role": "Whoever is putting this in front of the team and will be paged when it misbehaves"
    },
    {
      "name": "Platform / networking",
      "role": "Owns whatever proxy or CDN sits in front of the origin — that layer decides two of these four ceilings"
    },
    {
      "name": "Security reviewer",
      "role": "Needs to sign off that participant links are capabilities, not an audit trail"
    }
  ],
  "visibility": "open",
  "closeWhen": {
    "kind": "all"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "widget": "summary",
      "props": {
        "title": "Read this before the deploy, not after it",
        "lead": "Four constraints govern whether this service behaves once other people depend on it. Each was found in production rather than anticipated in design, which is why they are worth a page: none of them announces itself at deploy time, and three of them fail silently — everything looks fine while the guarantee is gone.",
        "points": [
          "Run exactly one replica. Replay protection does not degrade under horizontal scaling; it stops working.",
          "Long polls die at about 100 seconds behind Cloudflare. Holding a connection past the ceiling does not make the caller wait longer, it makes the wait fail.",
          "A CDN may cache by file extension regardless of what you send. The failure mode is agents installing the previous client for four hours after every deploy.",
          "Participant links are capabilities. A forwarded link is a forwarded identity. This is deliberate, and it is not an audit trail."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "findings",
      "props": {
        "title": "The four constraints in detail",
        "findings": [
          {
            "tone": "danger",
            "title": "Run exactly one replica",
            "detail": "The replay-protection nonce store is in memory (src/adapters/crypto/nonces.ts). Every signed request carries a nonce accepted once, which is what stops a captured request being replayed inside its five-minute freshness window. With two replicas, a nonce burned on one is unknown to the other, and a replayed request routed to the second one succeeds. The Deployment therefore pins replicas: 1 with a Recreate strategy — the single most important operational constraint here. Scaling out is possible; it means moving the nonce store to Postgres or Redis first. The moment replicas changes, that is the thing that has to change with it."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "Long polls die at about 100 seconds behind Cloudflare",
            "detail": "Cloudflare gives an origin roughly 100 seconds to respond before it answers 524 itself. Measured through the tunnel: a 150-second poll died at 125 seconds with HTTP 524 and no body. Not a slow response — no response, and no way for the caller to tell a dead wait from an empty one. MAX_AWAIT_MS therefore defaults to 90 seconds: every poll returns inside the window, and one that ran out its budget comes back with timedOut: true and whatever has arrived so far. A timeout is a legitimate outcome, not an error; the MCP client loops shorter polls, so a caller asking for five minutes gets five minutes. Behind a different proxy, find its own timeout and set MAX_AWAIT_MS below it. Whatever sits in front of this service decides the real ceiling; the service only decides whether it respects it."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "A CDN may cache by file extension regardless of what you send",
            "detail": "/mcp.js was served with cache-control: no-store and came back from the edge with cf-cache-status: HIT and an injected max-age=14400. Cloudflare caches .js by default, by extension, whatever the origin intended. For four hours after every deploy, agents installed the previous client. The fix is in the path, not in the headers: the canonical URL for the MCP client is /mcp, with no extension. /mcp.js remains as an alias for anyone who saved the old URL. If you put any CDN in front of this, check what it caches. Origin headers are a request, not an instruction, and the failure here is silent — everything works, one deploy behind."
          },
          {
            "tone": "info",
            "title": "Participant links are capabilities",
            "detail": "Anyone holding a participant link can answer as that participant. A forwarded link is a forwarded identity. This is deliberate and it is the reason the product works at all: participants need no account, no email verification, and no Claude access. Attribution is whichever link was used, which costs nothing and is honest about what it is. It is not an audit trail — do not deploy this expecting one. If responses ever need to be contested, that is the point to add verified identity, and that is a change to this service, not a configuration option waiting to be switched on. What you do control is the window: LINK_TTL_HOURS bounds how long a link stays answerable, and expiry is checked in the query that resolves a token rather than after it. The schema also carries revoked_at and honours it on every resolve, but nothing exposes revocation over HTTP yet, so killing one link today is an update against participant_links."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "key-facts",
      "props": {
        "title": "The settings these constraints translate into",
        "facts": [
          {
            "key": "replicas",
            "value": "1, with strategy Recreate. Not a tuning parameter."
          },
          {
            "key": "Nonce store",
            "value": "In memory (src/adapters/crypto/nonces.ts). Must move to Postgres or Redis before replicas > 1."
          },
          {
            "key": "Nonce freshness window",
            "value": "5 minutes — the window a captured request could be replayed in if the nonce check is bypassed."
          },
          {
            "key": "MAX_AWAIT_MS",
            "value": "90000 by default. Must sit below the timeout of whatever proxy is in front."
          },
          {
            "key": "Measured Cloudflare ceiling",
            "value": "~100s documented; a 150s poll died at 125s with HTTP 524 and no body."
          },
          {
            "key": "Canonical MCP client URL",
            "value": "/mcp — no extension. /mcp.js is an alias only."
          },
          {
            "key": "Observed bad cache",
            "value": "cf-cache-status: HIT with injected max-age=14400 (4 hours) on /mcp.js despite cache-control: no-store."
          },
          {
            "key": "LINK_TTL_HOURS",
            "value": "Bounds how long a participant link stays answerable. Expiry is checked inside the token-resolution query."
          },
          {
            "key": "Revocation",
            "value": "revoked_at exists in the schema and is honoured on every resolve, but is not exposed over HTTP. Killing a link today is a manual update against participant_links."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "comparison",
      "props": {
        "title": "What each constraint looks like when you get it wrong",
        "columns": [
          "Constraint",
          "Failure mode",
          "Does it announce itself?"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "replicas > 1",
            "Replayed signed requests succeed on the replica that never saw the nonce",
            "No — the service looks healthy and faster"
          ],
          [
            "MAX_AWAIT_MS above the proxy ceiling",
            "HTTP 524 with no body; caller cannot distinguish a dead wait from an empty one",
            "Partly — you get an error, but not a useful one"
          ],
          [
            "CDN caching the client by extension",
            "Agents install the previous client for the cache lifetime after every deploy",
            "No — everything works, one deploy behind"
          ],
          [
            "Treating links as identity",
            "A forwarded link answers as the original participant, indistinguishably",
            "No — attribution looks correct and is not contestable"
          ]
        ],
        "caption": "Three of these four are silent. That is the argument for checking them before the deploy rather than after the incident."
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tone": "warn",
      "text": "If any answer below is 'not checked yet', that is a fine answer — it just means the deploy waits until it isn't."
    },
    {
      "widget": "survey",
      "props": {
        "title": "1. Replica count and the nonce store",
        "intro": "This is the one that cannot be traded off. Replay protection is not degraded by a second replica; it is absent.",
        "questions": [
          {
            "kind": "select",
            "id": "replica_state",
            "label": "What does the manifest you are about to apply say?",
            "required": true,
            "options": [
              {
                "value": "pinned_one",
                "label": "replicas: 1 with strategy Recreate — confirmed by reading the manifest"
              },
              {
                "value": "one_but_unpinned",
                "label": "Effectively one replica, but nothing prevents an autoscaler or an operator raising it"
              },
              {
                "value": "more_than_one",
                "label": "More than one replica, or an HPA is attached"
              },
              {
                "value": "not_checked",
                "label": "Not checked yet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "textarea",
            "id": "replica_guard",
            "label": "What stops someone scaling this out in six months without knowing why they shouldn't? (A comment in the manifest, an admission policy, a runbook line — say which.)",
            "required": true
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "survey",
      "props": {
        "title": "2. The proxy in front, and MAX_AWAIT_MS",
        "intro": "Whatever sits in front of this service decides the real ceiling. The service only decides whether it respects it — so we need the number from your edge, not from ours.",
        "questions": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "proxy_name",
            "label": "What is actually in front of the origin in this environment? (Cloudflare tunnel, ALB, nginx, Envoy, nothing…)",
            "required": true
          },
          {
            "kind": "number",
            "id": "proxy_timeout_s",
            "label": "That proxy's response timeout, in seconds. Measured if you can, documented if you must.",
            "required": true
          },
          {
            "kind": "select",
            "id": "timeout_evidence",
            "label": "How do you know that number?",
            "required": true,
            "options": [
              {
                "value": "measured",
                "label": "Measured — I held a poll open until it died and recorded the time and status"
              },
              {
                "value": "documented",
                "label": "From the vendor's or our own configuration, not tested"
              },
              {
                "value": "assumed",
                "label": "Assumed from the default"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "number",
            "id": "max_await_ms",
            "label": "The MAX_AWAIT_MS you will deploy with, in milliseconds. It must sit comfortably below the number above.",
            "required": true
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "survey",
      "props": {
        "title": "3. Edge caching of the MCP client",
        "intro": "Origin headers are a request, not an instruction. The only reliable check is to fetch the client through the edge after a deploy and read the response headers.",
        "questions": [
          {
            "kind": "select",
            "id": "cdn_checked",
            "label": "Have you fetched the canonical client URL through the edge and inspected the cache headers?",
            "required": true,
            "options": [
              {
                "value": "checked_clean",
                "label": "Yes — no cache HIT, no injected max-age on /mcp"
              },
              {
                "value": "checked_dirty",
                "label": "Yes — it is being cached, and I have written down what needs changing"
              },
              {
                "value": "no_cdn",
                "label": "There is no CDN in this path at all"
              },
              {
                "value": "not_checked",
                "label": "Not checked yet"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "textarea",
            "id": "cdn_detail",
            "label": "Paste the cache-relevant response headers you saw, or say what you will check and when.",
            "required": false
          },
          {
            "kind": "boolean",
            "id": "canonical_url_distributed",
            "label": "Is the URL you are handing to your team the extensionless /mcp (not /mcp.js)?",
            "required": true
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "approval",
      "props": {
        "title": "4. Sign-off: participant links are capabilities",
        "proposal": "Deploy with participant links as bearer capabilities: anyone holding a link can answer as that participant, attribution is whichever link was used, and there is no audit trail.",
        "detail": "This is not a gap to be closed by configuration — it is the design that lets participants answer with no account, no email verification and no Claude access. Approving means accepting that a forwarded link is a forwarded identity, and that if a response is ever contested there is nothing to appeal to. Rejecting means verified identity has to be built into the service before this carries the kind of decision that gets contested. Approving with changes usually means tightening LINK_TTL_HOURS, restricting who links are sent to, or building the revocation endpoint that the schema is already ready for."
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "survey",
      "props": {
        "title": "The link window you will actually deploy with",
        "intro": "LINK_TTL_HOURS is the one control you have over the capability window, so it is worth choosing rather than inheriting.",
        "questions": [
          {
            "kind": "number",
            "id": "link_ttl_hours",
            "label": "LINK_TTL_HOURS for this deployment",
            "required": true
          },
          {
            "kind": "textarea",
            "id": "revocation_plan",
            "label": "Revocation is a manual UPDATE against participant_links today. Who can run that, and how would they find out a link needs killing?",
            "required": true
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "divider"
    },
    {
      "widget": "pick-one",
      "props": {
        "title": "The actual decision",
        "context": "All four constraints are known and none of them is a blocker on its own — but 'known' and 'checked in this environment' are different states, and only one of them survives a deploy.",
        "question": "Given your answers above, does this go live for the team now?",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "ship",
            "label": "Ship it — all four checked in this environment, settings match"
          },
          {
            "value": "ship_with_followups",
            "label": "Ship it, with named follow-ups tracked (say which, below)"
          },
          {
            "value": "hold",
            "label": "Hold — at least one constraint is unverified here"
          },
          {
            "value": "hold_needs_work",
            "label": "Hold — something needs building first (external nonce store, revocation endpoint, verified identity)"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "ask",
      "props": {
        "title": "Anything the next person needs and this page does not say",
        "context": "Each of these four was found rather than anticipated. If you find a fifth, this is where it starts.",
        "field": {
          "kind": "textarea",
          "id": "fifth_constraint",
          "label": "What did you hit in your environment that isn't written down above?",
          "required": false
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
